-- title:
--   Every town of the scale step is sparsely populated
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$, an aperture $\vartheta$, a radius $R_0$ and a number $L$ of reference cones,
--   and put
--
--   $$\delta(d,\vartheta) \;=\; \frac{\sqrt{d} + 1}{\sin(\vartheta/2)} \qquad (\texttt{apexShrinkConst } d\ \vartheta),$$
--
--   the constant of Lemma 5.9. A **scale step** for these data (Lean: `Δ : ScaleStep d ϑ R₀ L`) is a
--   natural number $\Delta$ that is even, satisfies $\delta(d,\vartheta) < \Delta$ and $R_0 < \Delta$,
--   and is divisible by $L$. Write $\Delta$ also for its underlying value `Δ.val`, viewed as a real
--   number, and assume $\Delta > 0$.
--
--   A pair of scales $(h,\ell)$ is **$\vartheta$-sparsely populated** when
--   $\delta(d,\vartheta) < h/\ell$ (`SparselyPopulated d ϑ h ℓ`). The claim is: for every natural
--   number $n$,
--
--   $$\frac{\sqrt{d} + 1}{\sin(\vartheta/2)} \;<\; \frac{\Delta^{\,n+1}}{\Delta^{\,n}},$$
--
--   that is, `SparselyPopulated d ϑ (Δ^(n+1)) (Δ^n)` holds: the town at the coarse scale
--   $\Delta^{n+1}$ and fine scale $\Delta^{n}$ is $\vartheta$-sparsely populated.
--
--   The point of choosing the scale step as a bundled datum is exactly this conclusion: it holds
--   simultaneously at every level $n$ of the hierarchy of scales, with the *same* $\vartheta$, so the
--   renormalisation argument can be run at each scale without re-choosing constants. Sparse population
--   at consecutive powers of $\Delta$ is what lets the chaining construction treat one town at a time.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The only hypothesis beyond the bundled `ScaleStep` datum is that its value
--   is strictly positive as a real number; it is what makes the quotient $\Delta^{n+1}/\Delta^{n}$ equal
--   $\Delta$, so the pair $(\Delta^{n+1}, \Delta^{n})$ always has ratio exactly $\Delta$, and the
--   conclusion is then the field `Δ.gt_delta`. The evenness of $\Delta$, the bound $R_0 < \Delta$ and
--   the divisibility $L \mid \Delta$ are carried by the datum but play no role here.
